        ('**B.** → False\n\nThe semi-log identity shows that the slope equals the force alone.\n\n$$\\ln P(t)=\\ln P_0+kt$$\n\nThe coefficient of $t$ is $k$. Claiming slope $P_0$ confuses the intercept $\\ln P_0$ with the slope letter. The intercept carries $P_0$; the slope does not.\n\nSemi-log geometry is the affine identity\n\n$$\\ln P(t)=\\ln P_0+kt$$\n\nChord slopes equal $k$, and that fact decides whether the claim fails.\n\nSo the statement is False.'),
        ('**C.** → True\n\nEvaluate the semi-log line at the initial time.\n\n$$\\ln P(0)=\\ln(P_0 e^{0})=\\ln P_0$$\n\nEquivalently, set $t=0$ in $\\ln P=\\ln P_0+kt$ to read the intercept $\\ln P_0$ directly. That is exactly the claim.\n\nSemi-log geometry is the affine identity\n\n$$\\ln P(t)=\\ln P_0+kt$$\n\nChord slopes equal $k$, and that fact decides whether the claim holds.\n\nSo the statement is True.'),
        ('**D.** → False\n\nAny chord of the affine log path has the same slope as the line itself.\n\n$$\\frac{\\ln P(t_2)-\\ln P(t_1)}{t_2-t_1}=\\frac{k(t_2-t_1)}{t_2-t_1}=k$$\n\nThe identity holds for every window with $t_2\\neq t_1$, not merely for the special unit window $[0,1]$.\n\nSemi-log geometry is the affine identity\n\n$$\\ln P(t)=\\ln P_0+kt$$\n\nChord slopes equal $k$, and that fact decides whether the claim fails.\n\nSo the statement is False.'),
        ("**E.** → False\n\nDifferentiate the continuous path twice when $k>0$.\n\n$$P'(t)=k P_0 e^{kt}$$\n\n$$P''(t)=k^{2} P_0 e^{kt}>0$$\n\nA strictly convex exponential curve is not a straight line on the ordinary $(t,P)$-plane. Semi-log linearity never upgrades to raw linearity when $k\\neq 0$.\n\nSo the statement is False."),
