        ('**C.** → True\n\nFor $t>T$ insert the second piece and take the natural log.\n\n$$P(t)=P_0 e^{k_1 T}e^{k_2(t-T)}$$\n\n$$\\ln P(t)=\\ln P_0+k_1 T+k_2(t-T)$$\n\nThat is exactly the claimed affine expression on the later interval.\n\nMatch values at the switch, then apply the later force.\n\n$$P(t)=P_0 e^{k_1 T}e^{k_2(t-T)}\\qquad(t>T)$$\n\nThe piecewise formula is what the claim must respect, and under it the claim holds.\n\nSo the statement is True.'),
        ('**D.** → False\n\nOn the first piece the average force over $[0,T]$ is the log increment divided by the length.\n\n$$\\frac{1}{T}\\ln\\frac{P(T)}{P_0}=\\frac{1}{T}\\ln\\frac{P_0 e^{k_1 T}}{P_0}=k_1$$\n\nThe recovered average equals $k_1$, not the later force $k_2$.\n\nMatch values at the switch, then apply the later force.\n\n$$P(t)=P_0 e^{k_1 T}e^{k_2(t-T)}\\qquad(t>T)$$\n\nThe piecewise formula is what the claim must respect, and under it the claim fails.\n\nSo the statement is False.'),
